WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.509
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:10.389
The title of this paper is very descriptive,
it says Controllable Characters Extracted

00:00:10.389 --> 00:00:12.240
from Real-World Videos.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.560
This sounds a little like science fiction,
so let’s pick this apart.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.650
If we forget about the controllable part,
we get something that you’ve seen in this

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:24.210
series many times - pose estimation.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:29.329
Pose estimation means that we have a human
character in an image or a video, have a computer

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:34.400
program look at it, and tell us the current
position this character is taking.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:40.370
This is useful for medical applications, such
as detecting issues with motor functionality,

00:00:40.370 --> 00:00:46.280
fall detection, or we can also use it for
motion capture for our video games and blockbuster

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:47.280
movies.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:53.699
So just performing the pose estimation part
is a great invention, but relatively old news.

00:00:53.699 --> 00:00:55.630
So what’s really new here?

00:00:55.630 --> 00:00:57.600
Why is this work interesting?

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.000
How does it go beyond pose estimation?

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.130
Well, as a hint, the title contains an additional
word, “controllable”, so, look at this!

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.410
Woo-hoo!

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:15.000
As you see, this technique is not only able
to identify where a character is, but we can

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.110
grab a controller, and move it around!

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:23.450
This means making this character perform novel
actions, and showing it from novel views.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:28.550
That’s really remarkable, because this requires
a proper understanding of the video we’re

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:29.550
watching.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.640
And this means that we can not only watch
these real-world videos, as you see this small

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:38.880
piece of footage used for the learning, but
by performing these actions with a controller,

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:48.970
we can make a video game out of it.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:52.340
Especially given that here, the background
has also been changed.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:56.940
To achieve this, this work contains two key
elements.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:01.630
Element number one is the pose2pose network
that takes an input posture and the button

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:06.540
we pushed on the controller, and creates the
next step of the animation.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:11.730
And then, element number two, the pose2frame
architecture then blends this new animation

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.709
step into an already existing image.

00:02:14.709 --> 00:02:19.480
The neural network that performs this is trained
in a way where it is encouraged to create

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:24.910
these character masks in a way that is continuous
and doesn’t contain jarring jumps between

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:29.040
the individual frames, leading to smooth and
believable movements.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:35.260
Now, clearly, anyone who takes a cursory look
sees that the animations are not perfect and

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:40.609
still contain artifacts, but just imagine
that this paper is among the first introductory

00:02:40.609 --> 00:02:43.230
works on this problem.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:45.659
Imagine what we’ll have two more papers
down the line.

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:46.959
I can’t wait.

00:02:46.959 --> 00:02:50.769
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

